It possesses two complex conjugate zeros near \\(-1.0297\\pm 0.1097\\,i\\).\n\n**Remark 5.4**  We were unable to find counterexamples for the logarithmic concavity of \\(Q_M(x)\\) and \\(Y_M(x)\\) using matroids of the form described above. We have verified with the help of a computer that Conjecture 1.2 (c) and (d) hold for all corank 2 matroids of cardinality less than or equal to 35.\n\n**References**\n\n[AS23] Federico Ardila and Mario Sanchez, *Valuations and the Hopf Monoid of Generalized Permutahedra*, Int. Math. Res. Not. IMRN (2023), no. 5, 4149–4224. 4, 15\n\n[BHM+22] Tom Braden, June Huh, Jacob P. Matherne, Nicholas Proudfoot, and Botong Wang, *Singular Hodge theory for combinatorial geometries*, arXiv e-prints (2022), arXiv:2010.06088. 1, 2, 5\n\n[BV20] Tom Braden and Artem Vysochorets, *Kazhdan-Lusztig polynomials of matroids under deletion*, Electron. J. Combin. 27 (2020), no. 1, Paper No. 1.17, 17. 2, 3, 5, 6, 7, 13--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nMaximal matroids in weak order posets - ScienceDirect (https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0095895623000928)\nciteturn7search0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3 months ago; Author links open overlay panel Bill Jackson ^{a}, Shin-ichi Tanigawa ^{b} ... The conjecture suggests a new type of matroid rank function which extends the concept of weakly saturated sequences from extremal graph theory. ... Submodularity\n\n## Journal of Combinatorial Theory, Series B\n\nDate: March 2024\n\nPages: 20-46\n\nVolume: Volume 165\n\n## Published by: Elsevier\n\n# Maximal matroids in weak order posets\n\nAuthor links open overlay panel Bill Jackson ^{a}, Shin-ichi Tanigawa ^{b}\n\nShow more\n\nView PDF Download full issueView Open Manuscript\n\nCite\n\nAdd to Mendeley\n\nShare\n\n10.1016/j.jctb.2023.10.012\n\nMore actions\n\n  * Article\n\n## Abstract\n\nLet \\mathcal{X} be a family of subsets of a finite set E. A matroid on E is called an \\mathcal{X}-matroid if each set in \\mathcal{X} is a circuit. We develop techniques for determining when there exists a unique maximal \\mathcal{X}-matroid in the weak order poset of all \\mathcal{X}-matroids on E and formulate a conjecture which would characterise the rank function of this unique maximal matroid when it exists. The conjecture suggests a new type of matroid rank function which extends the concept of weakly saturated sequences from extremal graph theory. We verify the conjecture for various families \\mathcal{X} and show that, if true, the conjecture could have important applications in such areas as combinatorial rigidity and low rank matrix completion.\n\n## Keywords\n\nMatroid on a graph\n\n;\n\nMatroid weak order\n\n;\n\nUnique maximal matroid\n\n;\n\nMatroid erection\n\n;\n\nRank function\n\n;\n\nSubmodularity\n\n;\n\nWeakly saturated sequence\n\n;\n\nCombinatorial rigidity\n\n## Data availability--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCounting Bases (https://www.collectionscanada.ca/obj/s4/f2/dsk3/OWTU/TC-OWTU-446.pdf)\nciteturn7search14 [wordlim: 200] Published: 7 months ago; The matrix pair (A1, A2) then has a Pfaﬃan signing if an only if there exists a vector x ... For graphic matroid pairs, Little’s Theorem [22] (Section 3.3) implies the much ... We conjecture that this general linear bound for certifying that a matroid pair is\nthe set of bases of P. However, since K has linear rank, this formulation can be used to\nsuccinctly certify that a matroid pair is non-Pfaﬃan.\nA set C ⊆B(P) is called inconsistent with respect to α for P if there is no set X ⊆S\nsuch that |X ∩B| ≡αB mod 2 for all B ∈C. It follows that if P has an inconsistent set\nwith respect to α and P has an inconsistent set with respect to α′, then P is non-Pfaﬃan.\nThe union of two such inconsistent sets is called a non-Pfaﬃan bases certiﬁcate. The\nfollowing theorem is proved in Section 3.4:\nTheorem 1.2. If P = (M1, M2) is a matroid pair on the ground set